35:The corpus callosum, a crucial white matter structure connecting the left and right hemispheres of the brain, is essential for interhemispheric communication  and  the  integration  of  cognitive  functions  (Schmied et al., 2020; Ganjavi et al., 2011). Studies on individuals with split-brain and agenesis of the corpus callosum have attributed corpus callosum to a wide  range  of  brain  functions,  most  commonly  somatosensory,  language, and memory functions (Blackwood and Tubbs, 2023). The corpus callosum  plays  a  crucial  role  in  integrating  information  related  to perception  and  attention,  which  primarily  develops  during  the  preschool years (de Haan et al., 2020; Gardier and Geurten, 2024; Raile et al., 2020; Ross et al., 2020).
51:This study investigated early childhood corpus callosum development, a critical process for cognitive maturation and implicated in Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), using sex-specific growth curve models. Structural MRI data from  295  typically  developing  children  (TDC;  aged  1 -6  years)  were  used  to  model  age-  and  sex-dependent changes in ten morphometric parameters, including subregion volumes and midsagittal plane features. Analyses  revealed  nonlinear  developmental  trajectories,  region-specific  growth  rates,  and  earlier  developmental peaks in females. We applied these normative models to an independent dataset of 41 TDC and 26 children with ASD,  acquired  on  a  different  scanner.  Classifiers  trained  on  deviations  from  the  growth  curves  accurately distinguished children with ASD from TDC (mean Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve [AUC] = 0.95),  demonstrating  model  generalizability.  These  findings  establish  sex-specific  corpus  callosum  growth curve models as a quantitative, generalizable tool for characterizing typical development and detecting atypical morphometry,  offering  a  promising  approach  for  early,  objective  ASD  diagnosis  and  potentially  facilitating timely intervention. Further study of model generalizability across more diverse populations is warranted.
53:mental  health.  For  instance,  toddlers  with  autism  spectrum  disorder (ASD)  aged  2 -4  years  have  been  shown  to  exhibit  increased  corpus callosum  volumes  compared  to  typically  developing  children  (Zhang et al., 2023). Longitudinal studies also suggest that the atypical development of the corpus callosum ' s white matter microstructure in ASD begins in early childhood and persists into adolescence and adulthood (Courchesne et al., 2011).
55:The  corpus  callosum  exhibits  a  nonlinear  developmental  pattern, with  different  regions  showing  distinct  growth  trajectories  during maturation. The most significant changes occur in the early years of life, stabilizing after adolescence (Tanaka-Arakawa et al., 2015; Chavarria et al., 2014). A longitudinal study by Tsuzuki et al. found that the growth rate of the midsection of the corpus callosum is faster during the second year of infancy compared to the first year (Tsuzuki et al., 2022). In a study of 118 children aged 1 -18.7 years, Vannucci et al. discovered that the development of the genu (the anterior part of the corpus callosum) is
59:significantly faster than that of the splenium (the posterior part) during the first six years of life (Vannucci et al., 2016).
61:However,  we  still  lack  a  clear  understanding  of  how  the  typical features of corpus callosum morphology change with age in children. While some studies have included younger age ranges (Schmied et al., 2020), they often used coarse group comparison methods, grouping by broader age intervals for differential testing, or focused solely on ages below two years. Consequently, these studies have not illustrated the year-by-year changes in corpus callosum morphological indicators.
63:This study aims to construct growth curve models to characterize the morphological  features  of  the  corpus  callosum  in  children  aged  1 -6 years. By elucidating the growth rate and inter-individual variability of ten  fine-grained  morphometrics  of  the  corpus  callosum,  we  seek  to provide insights into the timing and pace of corpus callosum maturation during early childhood. Furthermore, we aim to examine the hypothesis that the these growth curve models are effective in identifying atypical development associated ASD. If the hypothesis is proved, this approach could lead to a novel tool for quantitatively recognizing corpus callosum abnormalities and assessing developmental deviations.
