## 2.4. Image quality check

We first used the CAT12 toolbox (Gaser et al., 2024) to evaluate the quality of all images. According to the CAT quality report interpretation, the quality report rates image quality IQR (Image Quality Rating) based on resolution, noise, and bias into 16 grades from A + to F. Since this study focuses on the corpus callosum and the pediatric data is extremely valuable, to ensure sufficient modeling data, we chose an IQR equal or less  than ' C-' as  the  exclusion  criterion,  which  excluded  12  images. Subsequently, we manually inspected the remaining image data using ITK-SNAP (Yushkevich et al., 2006) to ensure no significant artifacts, noise, or unclear contours in the corpus callosum region. This process excluded 6 additional images. The remaining images had an average IQR of 82.55 % (above B-, with a standard deviation of 2.70 %).

Table 1 summarizes the sample sizes for all datasets, after image quality control. Since percentiles from Dataset 2 were extracted through the model to validate its ability to identify ASD patients, there was no need to differentiate between age groups.

Table 1

Overview of Datasets.

| Category          | Female   | Male   | Total   |
|-------------------|----------|--------|---------|
| Dataset 1         |          |        |         |
| Age Group 1       | 27       | 33     | 60      |
| Age Group 2       | 22       | 34     | 56      |
| Age Group 3       | 27       | 22     | 49      |
| Age Group 4       | 15       | 30     | 45      |
| Age Group 5       | 19       | 28     | 47      |
| Age Group 6       | 28       | 20     | 48      |
| Total (Dataset 1) | 138      | 157    | 295     |


